Title: Judge's Crossword Puzzle No. 74
Author: Catherine McMillan
Date: 1928-10-20
Byline: Submitted by Catherine McMillan, Chicago, Ill.
Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1928-10-20_95/page/n31/mode/1up
Puzzle-Number: 74
Solution-Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1928-10-27_95/page/n31/mode/1up


MY###PRESS###NO
R.HS#LEMON#NOON
#ROT#.ISLE#END#
#DERBY###EDGES#
###ARM#R.ZOR###
UTOPI.#ADENOIDS
SOB#STAID###NET
ATE#KERNELS#AVE
GAS###SIREN#NIP
ELECTRON#MODELS
###OWING#ORE###
#BROOD###NESTS#
#OAK#DERMA#KIT#
PAWS#LAIRD#SEAT
IT###ER.SE###GO


A1. This indicates possession. ~ MY
A3. This puts things down in black and white. ~ PRESS
A8. One of the safest words to use. ~ NO
A10. What college boys make whoopee with. ~ R.HS
A13. Squeeze this and it hits you in the eye. ~ LEMON
A14. What causes two hands to go up in Chicago. ~ NOON
A16. You find a lot of this in the—Horizontal 3. ~ ROT
A17. Where there is standing room only. ~ .ISLE
A18. This is the limit. ~ END
A19. The crown of an American Big Chief. ~ DERBY
A21. These are on the border. ~ EDGES
A23. What Herbert Hoover has up his sleeve. ~ ARM
A24. Any chauffeur hates to have this cut in on him. ~ R.ZOR
A26. The Heaven of H G. Wells. ~ UTOPI.
A29. Cause of many of the faults of the younger generation. ~ ADENOIDS
A33. This gets results for the female of the species. ~ SOB
A34. This kind of person is steady and sober. ~ STAID
A36. This makes a catch. ~ NET
A37. What the cannibal chief did when he met the minister. ~ ATE
A38. These make an earful. ~ KERNELS
A41. This runs up and down in New York. (Abbr.) ~ AVE
A42. This is always getting tanked. ~ GAS
A43. This screams when there is trouble. ~ SIREN
A44. A Scotch drink. ~ NIP
A45. An electrified particle. ~ ELECTRON
A49. These make a bare living. ~ MODELS
A51. What people are doing over the month-end. ~ OWING
A52. Native of the underworld. ~ ORE
A53. This will have to scratch for a living. ~ BROOD
A55. Where the jays hang out. (Plural.) ~ NESTS
A58. This has a shady reputation. ~ OAK
A59. Greek skin. ~ DERMA
A63. What the plumber forgot. ~ KIT
A64. These come up to scratch. ~ PAWS
A65. A landlord that is Scotch. ~ LAIRD
A66. What a rising young man leaves behind him. ~ SEAT
A68. An over-used pronoun. ~ IT
A69. What to do when you make an error. ~ ER.SE
A70. Never do this when you see red. ~ GO

D1. An American title. ~ MR
D2. A sprinter's measure. ~ Y.RD
D3. This is companionate. ~ PL.YM.TE
D4. A Portuguese coin. ~ REI
D5. Printer's measures. ~ EMS
D6. An early western settler. ~ SOL
D7. A cold indicator. ~ SNEEZE
D8. Affirmative signs. ~ NODS
D9. Preposition. ~ ON
D11. This will be a help to the farmer. ~ HOE
D12. This is supposed to keep the kiddies on the Straight and Narrow—rather out of date now. ~ STRAP
D14. A race in Louisville, Kentucky. ~ NEGRO
D15. A number. ~ ONE
D20. This kind of person isn't so slow. ~ BRISK
D22. What Al Smith will do with his brown derby. ~ DON
D24. What cats and dogs are said to be doing every April. ~ RAINING
D25. What one who keeps the bridge score must be. ~ .DDER
D26. This gets to be a habit. ~ USAGE
D27. This is the whole thing. ~ TOTAL
D28. This describes a heavy weight. ~ OBESE
D30. This kind of person hasn't much sense. ~ INANE
D31. An underworld denizen. ~ DEVIL
D32. These carry a rising young man to the top. ~ STEPS
D35. This is a crime. ~ ARSON
D39. The suckers are always in this at the circus. ~ LEMONADE
D40. No wide-awake person would do this. ~ SNORE
D46. These are always stirring up something. ~ COOKS
D47. An even number. ~ TWO
D48. This will keep you guessing. ~ RIDDLE
D50. These are often left for baseball parks. ~ DESKS
D53. This gives better service when not tipped. ~ BOAT
D54. The kind of deal we hate to get. ~ RAW
D56. This gets run over by a railroad train. ~ TIE
D57. A deer daddy. ~ STAG
D60. A listening-in device. ~ EAR
D61. Royal Irish Academy. (Abbr.) ~ RI.
D62. The other half of Vertical 1. ~ MRS
D64. A printer's mixture. ~ PI
D67. Toward. ~ TO


